Tests first (RED): scripts/tests/daemon-json-cap.test.mjs. Edit only with your own editing tool (Claude Edit/Write), never a shell write (project customRules).
- Line 4: add readlinkSync to the node:fs import, alphabetical: `import { existsSync, mkdtempSync, readFileSync, readlinkSync, rmSync, statSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';`.
- Inside the `if (engine.ext === 'sh') {` block, insert the block below immediately before that block's `    continue;` (after the row 'identity: a live pid with an unreachable /health is adopted as today', :388-393 at 4c7d6464). It reuses the file's runCheck, withHealth, closedPort, healthy, assertRefused, running(port), OTHER and PROJECT.sh. The test names are fixed, because the gate counts them. Paste verbatim:

```js
    // daemon.json from another pid namespace (a Docker container; the daemon records
    // pidNamespace, task-da89f620): its pid means nothing here, so /health decides
    // (task-a7f8d8d6). Without an exact other namespace the kill -0 rule stays.
    const OWN_NS = (() => { try { return readlinkSync('/proc/self/ns/pid'); } catch { return null; } })();
    const DEAD_PID = 2147483646; // no process on any host: Linux pid_max is at most 2^22
    const nsInfo = (port, pidNamespace) => JSON.stringify({ pid: DEAD_PID, port, startedAt: 'x', projectPath: '/mnt/c/moe-id/proj', ...(pidNamespace === undefined ? {} : { pidNamespace }) });
    t('pid namespace: another namespace whose /health answers for this project is adopted, untouched', () =>
      withHealth(200, healthy('C:/moe-id/proj'), async (port, requests) => {
        const content = nsInfo(port, 'pid:[1]');
        const r = await runCheck(engine, content);
        assert.equal(r.status, 0, r.out);
        assert.ok(r.lines.includes(`[OK] Daemon reachable at 127.0.0.1:${port} (daemon.json from another pid namespace)`), r.out);
        assert.ok(r.lines.includes(running(port)), r.out);
        assert.ok(!r.out.includes('Stale daemon.json'), r.out);
        assert.equal(r.after, content);
        assert.deepEqual(requests, ['GET /health']);
      }));
    t('pid namespace: another namespace with no /health answer is removed as stale', async () => {
      const port = await closedPort();
      const r = await runCheck(engine, nsInfo(port, 'pid:[1]'));
      assert.equal(r.status, 0, r.out);
      assert.ok(r.lines.includes(`[INFO] Stale daemon.json detected (no /health answer at 127.0.0.1:${port} from its pid namespace)`), r.out);
      assert.ok(r.lines.includes(`@@ port=[${port}] project=[/mnt/c/moe-id/proj] running=false`), r.out);
      assert.equal(r.after, null);
    });
    t('pid namespace: another namespace whose daemon serves another project is refused, untouched', () =>
      withHealth(200, healthy(OTHER), async (port, requests) => {
        const content = nsInfo(port, 'pid:[1]');
        const r = await runCheck(engine, content);
        assertRefused(r, port, OTHER, PROJECT.sh);
        assert.equal(r.after, content);
        assert.deepEqual(requests, ['GET /health']);
      }));
    for (const [label, pidNamespace, skip] of [
      ['no pidNamespace', undefined],
      ["this host's own pidNamespace", OWN_NS, OWN_NS === null && 'no /proc/self/ns/pid on this host'],
      ["pidNamespace 'pid:[x]'", 'pid:[x]'],
      ["pidNamespace 'net:[1]'", 'net:[1]'],
      ['a 21-digit pidNamespace', `pid:[${'1'.repeat(21)}]`],
      ['a numeric pidNamespace', 1],
    ]) {
      t(`pid namespace: a dead pid with ${label} keeps the kill -0 rule (removed, no /health)`, () =>
        withHealth(200, healthy('C:/moe-id/proj'), async (port, requests) => {
          const r = await runCheck(engine, nsInfo(port, pidNamespace));
          assert.equal(r.status, 0, r.out);
          assert.ok(r.lines.includes(`[INFO] Stale daemon.json detected (PID ${DEAD_PID} is not running)`), r.out);
          assert.equal(r.after, null);
          assert.deepEqual(requests, []);
        }), engine.skip || skip);
    }
```

What the 9 rows (per sh engine) prove:
- Fixture: pid DEAD_PID 2147483646, the daemon tests' DEAD_PID, which is no process on any host. 'pid:[1]' is another namespace on every OS, because no real namespace inode is 1.
- Row 1 (DoD 1): /health answers healthy for this project. Expect exit 0, the line `[OK] Daemon reachable at 127.0.0.1:<port> (daemon.json from another pid namespace)`, `running=true`, no 'Stale daemon.json', daemon.json byte-identical and exactly one GET /health.
  - `running=true` is the 'starts no daemon' proof. SEGMENTS.sh.check ends at `if [ "$RUNNING" = false ]; then`, and in the real wrapper RUNNING=true skips that start block.
- Row 2: nothing listens on the port. Expect `[INFO] Stale daemon.json detected (no /health answer at 127.0.0.1:<port> from its pid namespace)`, running=false, and the file removed.
- Row 3: /health answers for another project. Expect the MOE_DAEMON_FOREIGN_PROJECT refusal (assertRefused, exit 1), with the file byte-identical.
- Rows 4-9 (DoD 2): a dead pid keeps today's kill -0 rule when pidNamespace is absent, this host's own, 'pid:[x]', 'net:[1]', 21 digits or numeric 1.
  - 'Own' is node's readlinkSync('/proc/self/ns/pid'). That row is skipped where no such link exists (Windows, macOS).
  - Each row expects `[INFO] Stale daemon.json detected (PID 2147483646 is not running)`, the file removed, and NO /health request. That proves the kill -0 path ran, not the probe.
- RED check before step 2, run in place in the worktree (the filtered rows need no dist/): `node --test --test-name-pattern 'pid namespace' scripts/tests/daemon-json-cap.test.mjs`.
  - Expect 3 failures, the 'another namespace' rows: the unfixed wrapper prints 'Stale daemon.json detected (PID 2147483646 is not running)' and deletes the file. Expect 6 passes.
  - The architect's dry run on a scratch copy got exactly that.
- Error handling: the rows only use the file's helpers. They close every fake server in finally and remove each temp dir, and start no process the harness does not already start.
